1://! Screen readers read the document (#488). The canvas draws pages as pictures, so on its own it
2://! is one opaque widget to assistive technology. This module describes the document under it as
3://! an AccessKit tree, the way a browser describes a page:
4://!
5://! - the canvas widget is the **document** node (named after the document), holding the caret
6://!   and selection as an AccessKit text selection;
7://! - each body paragraph is a **paragraph**, a **heading** (with its outline level) or a **list
8://!   item** (with its list level, grouped into lists), holding one **text run** per laid-out line
9://!   with its characters' screen positions, so a reader can follow the caret by character, word
10://!   and line and point at text, and a magnifier can keep the caret in view;
11://! - tables are **table → row → cell**, pictures and charts **images** named by their alt text,
12://!   hyperlinks **links** around their text runs; list numbers are **list markers**.
13://!
14://! Only the pages on screen and one page either side are described, and the tree is capped, so a
15://! long document costs no more than a short one. Nothing is built unless a screen reader (or a
16://! test) asked egui for AccessKit output; the tree is rebuilt only when the layout, the selection,
17://! the described pages or their place on screen changed.
